(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 66-year-old man with chronic aortic regurgitation has no symptoms, but his echocardiogram now shows a reduced ejection fraction. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Percutaneous balloon valvotomy","Evaluation as a surgical candidate","Continue afterload reduction alone","Wait until symptoms develop"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic aortic regurgitation with a mildly reduced or reduced ejection fraction, with or without symptoms, makes the patient a surgical candidate. Left untreated, survival is only about 2 to 3 years. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF and in the right-sided lead V4R, consistent with right ventricular infarction. His blood pressure is 104/66 mmHg. Which medication should be avoided?","choices":["A heparin drip","Nitroglycerin","Chewable aspirin","Clopidogrel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a right ventricular infarct, do not give nitroglycerin: it lowers preload, the failing right ventricle loses its Frank-Starling support, output falls further and the patient can go into shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 62, 63"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is recovering from an ST-elevation myocardial infarction. Several weeks later he develops pleuritic chest pain and a pericardial friction rub. Which complication of the infarction does this represent?","choices":["Ventricular aneurysm","Myocardial rupture","Thromboembolism","Pericarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis (Dressler syndrome) is listed among the mechanical complications of myocardial infarction. Pleuritic pain with a friction rub weeks after the infarct points to the pericardium rather than the ventricle. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Asymptomatic left ventricular hypertrophy places a patient in which heart failure stage?","choices":["Stage A","Stage C","Stage B","Stage D"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stage B is asymptomatic structural heart disease, and left ventricular hypertrophy counts as structural disease, like ischemic cardiomyopathy and systolic or diastolic dysfunction. Stage A requires a structurally normal heart. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes has asymptomatic left ventricular hypertrophy on echocardiogram. She already takes an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. Which drug class should be added for her diabetes-related risk?","choices":["Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Calcium channel blocker such as amlodipine","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. She is stage B, and stage B carries a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor for patients with diabetes as well as the converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Spironolactone and eplerenone belong to which group?","choices":["Thiazide-like diuretics","Potassium-wasting loop diuretics","Sodium-glucose inhibitors","Potassium-sparing receptor antagonists"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone and eplerenone are mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists, the potassium-sparing diuretics, and a strong recommendation in reduced ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia has progressive atherosclerosis despite maximal drug therapy and apheresis. Which referral may help by supplying the receptors he lacks?","choices":["Kidney transplantation","Liver transplantation","Carotid ultrasound only","Bariatric surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Liver transplantation may prove helpful in the homozygous disorder by providing low-density lipoprotein receptors that his own liver lacks or cannot use. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with known dilated cardiomyopathy has worsening breathlessness. Blood pressure is 102/64, a third heart sound (an extra sound in early diastole just after the second heart sound) is present, and he has crackles. Which ejection fraction is the echocardiogram expected to show?","choices":["About 45%","About 25%","About 60%","About 75%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dilated cardiomyopathy is the systolic chain: poor contraction, a reduced ejection fraction of 40% or less, low-normal pressure, a third heart sound and pulmonary edema. A value near 25% fits. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has weight loss, tremor, heat intolerance and palpitations for two months, and now ankle edema and breathlessness. Heart rate is 124 and B-type natriuretic peptide is elevated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["High-output failure from hyperthyroidism","Diastolic failure from hypertension","Systolic failure from ischemic disease","Right-sided failure from mitral stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hyperthyroidism raises peripheral demand, and the heart answers with a faster rate and stronger contraction until the heart failure syndrome appears: fatigue, dyspnea, edema and palpitations. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What produces the pressure drop in non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["A vagal surge with sudden bradycardia","A rapid ventricular rhythm on standing","Low output or weak vasoconstriction","Failed norepinephrine release from nerves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reduced cardiac output and/or impaired vasoconstriction without a primary autonomic disorder leaves the brain poorly perfused on standing. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with two hours of chest pressure has a high-sensitivity troponin I of 16 ng/L. How should this value be interpreted?","choices":["Abnormal only in a man","Too low to report","Abnormal for a woman","Normal for any adult"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The 99th-percentile (abnormal) high-sensitivity troponin I value is 12 ng/L for women and 20 ng/L for men. At 16 ng/L, her high-sensitivity troponin I is abnormal. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 20-year-old woman has a classic history of palpitations and lightheadedness on standing that improve lying down, but her tilt-table test is negative. How should the negative result be interpreted?","choices":["It does not exclude the syndrome","It points to autonomic failure","It rules the syndrome out","It points to a cardiac cause"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With a sensitivity of about 40%, many patients with the syndrome test negative, so a negative result tells almost nothing; a positive result, with specificity above 80%, carries more weight. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has severe mitral regurgitation. Which electrocardiogram finding is most expected?","choices":["An expected normal tracing","Right axis deviation","Right atrial enlargement","Left atrial enlargement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe mitral regurgitation shows left atrial enlargement and left ventricular hypertrophy on the electrocardiogram, though with low sensitivity and questionable specificity. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 48-year-old Black man develops lip and tongue swelling three months after starting an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. What should be known about this?","choices":["A beta blocker is the appropriate substitution","It is two to four times more common in this group","An angiotensin receptor blocker carries no risk whatsoever","It is a dose-related effect that resolves on dose reduction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is a bradykinin-mediated class effect of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibition. Angiotensin receptor blockers can usually be used instead, though angioedema with them remains possible. An angiotensin receptor blocker is usually substituted. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a fasting low-density lipoprotein of 215 mg/dL on repeat testing. Her thyroid function, kidney function and urinalysis are normal, and she has no atherosclerotic disease. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["High-intensity statin","Coronary artery calcium scoring","Lifestyle alone for six months","Moderate-intensity statin after risk calculation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-density lipoprotein of 190 mg/dL or more is severe primary hypercholesterolemia: begin a high-intensity statin without calculating 10-year risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman on amlodipine reports swollen ankles that are worse by evening. Her jugular venous pressure is normal and her lungs are clear. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Heart failure requiring a loop diuretic","Hypoalbuminemia from thiazide therapy","An adverse effect of renin-angiotensin blockade","Dependent ankle edema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It follows from arterial vasodilation rather than fluid overload, which is why a diuretic does not fix it. Flushing, headache and gingival enlargement belong to the same class. It is the commonest effect of a dihydropyridine. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman started a moderate-intensity statin six weeks ago for primary prevention. She feels well. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Recheck lipids in two years","Repeat the lipid panel now","Stop the statin, she feels well","Double the intensity without labs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adherence and percentage response are assessed with a repeat lipid panel 4 to 12 weeks after starting a statin, then every 3 to 12 months as needed; six weeks falls in that window. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with resistant hypertension is started on lisinopril. Her creatinine rises markedly within a week. What does this suggest?","choices":["Bilateral renal artery stenosis","Acute interstitial nephritis from the medication","Progression of underlying diabetic nephropathy","Expected drug effect requiring no change in approach"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Where perfusion depends on angiotensin-mediated efferent arteriolar tone, blocking it drops the filtration pressure sharply. This is one of the recognized clues to renovascular disease, and it is why the agent is contraindicated in bilateral stenosis. A hemodynamically significant unilateral stenosis does the same. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How does the heart rate behave in neurogenic orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["It falls sharply first","It rises significantly","It rises only minimally","It exceeds 120 per minute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Failed autonomic signaling means the heart rate rises less than expected even as pressure falls, which is the tell that separates it from volume loss. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which adverse effects and cautions attach to angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors?","choices":["Sedation, dry mouth and rebound hypertension on withdrawal","Dependent ankle edema, flushing and headache","Hyperuricemia precipitating gout","Dry cough, hyperkalemia, creatinine rise and angioedema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cough is bradykinin-mediated, which is why switching to an angiotensin receptor blocker usually resolves it while keeping the same therapeutic effect. They are avoided in pregnancy, in prior angioedema and in bilateral renal artery stenosis. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman is three days out from a myocardial infarction complicated by acute heart failure, with an ejection fraction of 30%. A colleague proposes an implantable defibrillator before discharge. Which step is premature at this point?","choices":["Optimizing her guideline therapy","Implanting a defibrillator now","Starting a wearable defibrillator","Referring to cardiac rehabilitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After acute heart failure from infarction, the pathway is a wearable defibrillator, optimized therapy and cardiac rehabilitation, then a repeat echocardiogram; the implantable device follows only if there is no improvement. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of tricuspid regurgitation?","choices":["Rheumatic scarring of the leaflets","Carcinoid disease of the valve","Endocarditis from intravenous drug use","Pulmonary hypertension dilating the annulus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation is most commonly due to dilation of the tricuspid annulus from right ventricular dilation in the setting of pulmonary hypertension. Primary disease is infective endocarditis in drug users. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"ST elevation in I, aVL, V5 and V6 points to which culprit artery?","choices":["Posterior interventricular branch","Left circumflex artery","Right coronary artery","Right marginal branch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lateral leads I, aVL, V5 and V6 point to the left circumflex artery or a diagonal branch of the left anterior descending artery. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 61, 62"},{"q":"Vasospastic angina typically occurs under which circumstance?","choices":["After a large meal","With deep breathing","At rest","During exertion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vasospastic angina occurs at rest, from coronary artery vasospasm in normal or diseased arteries, and responds to nitroglycerin. Most patients have their pain between midnight and early morning. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman standing at an outdoor event in the heat develops tunnel vision, lightheadedness and dizziness but is still conscious. If her pressure keeps falling, what is likely to follow?","choices":["A prolonged coma","A brief faint","A generalized seizure","Supine hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her symptoms are near-syncope, which precedes syncope: a transient, self-limited loss of consciousness followed by spontaneous recovery. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with confirmed resistant hypertension has had his diuretic optimized and his other two agents titrated to maximally tolerated doses. His filtration rate and potassium are normal. Which fourth agent has the most evidence?","choices":["A beta blocker","A second dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","Spironolactone","Hydralazine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, provided the filtration rate and potassium allow. It is added after the foundational three are optimized rather than instead of doing so. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has severe epigastric pain radiating to his back. Clusters of small yellowish-white papules (eruptive xanthomas) cover his buttocks and the backs of his arms, and fundoscopy shows opalescent retinal vessels (lipemia retinalis). His fasting triglyceride is 2100 mg/dL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Metabolic syndrome","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fasting triglyceride above 500 and usually above 1000 mg/dL, acute pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas and lipemia retinalis (sometimes with hepatosplenomegaly) define this syndrome, which can present in childhood or adulthood. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What level of high-density lipoprotein cholesterol is classified as low?","choices":["Below 150 mg/dL","Below 60 mg/dL","Below 40 mg/dL","Below 100 mg/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High-density lipoprotein below 40 mg/dL is low and counts as a major coronary risk factor, while 60 mg/dL or more is high and removes one risk factor from the count. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which outcome is associated with consuming fatty fish or fish-oil supplements?","choices":["More low-density lowering than statins","Lower risk of coronary death","Higher high-density lipoprotein than niacin","Cure of familial hypercholesterolemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fatty fish or fish-oil supplements are associated with lower risk of coronary heart disease and coronary death, may lower thrombotic stroke risk, and at least 2 servings of fish a week are advised. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man is newly diagnosed with hypertension. Which baseline studies are obtained?","choices":["A chest radiograph and echocardiogram in every patient","Plasma free metanephrines in every patient","Creatinine alone, with nothing further","Renal function, electrolytes, glucose, lipids and urinalysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio alongside the creatinine. The panel does three jobs at once: screens for secondary causes, measures organ damage, and establishes a baseline before drugs that alter potassium and renal function are started. Thyroid-stimulating hormone and a full blood count complete it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has pulmonic regurgitation on echocardiogram and has developed leg edema and distended neck veins. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Cardiology referral","Repeat echo in 5 years","Start lifelong warfarin","No follow-up needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic regurgitation is referred when it is seen on echocardiogram or when there are right heart failure signs and symptoms, both of which she has. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man admitted with acute heart failure has diuresed well. On the day of planned discharge his B-type natriuretic peptide is 360. What does this predischarge level suggest?","choices":["Higher risk of 30-day readmission","The level carries no prognostic value","Acute heart failure is still likely","Lower risk of 30-day readmission"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A predischarge level below 430 carries a lower risk of readmission within 30 days, which supports discharge planning. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which examination findings support a suspicion of aortic dissection?","choices":["Weak femoral pulses present since childhood","Normal and symmetric pulses, which exclude the diagnosis","Pulse deficits, asymmetric arm pressures or a new deficit","Bilateral leg edema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, but their absence proves nothing: absent pulse findings do not exclude the diagnosis, so a normal pulse examination must not close the question. An aortic regurgitation murmur belongs on the list too. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy has a harsh, high-pitched crescendo-decrescendo mid-systolic ejection murmur at the left second to fourth intercostal spaces at the sternal border. It grows louder with inspiration, and the second heart sound is split. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pulmonic stenosis","Pulmonic regurgitation","Tricuspid regurgitation","Aortic stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Harsh means stenosis and systolic narrows it to the aortic or pulmonic valve; the left upper sternal border, the inspiratory increase of a right-sided murmur and the split second sound mark pulmonic stenosis. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 57, 8"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from multiple system atrophy has supine hypertension (high blood pressure while lying flat). He remains symptomatic on standing despite slow position changes. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":["Slow position changes","Droxidopa","Fludrocortisone","Midodrine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Midodrine is best with neurogenic syncope but is avoided with supine hypertension, severe heart disease and chronic kidney disease. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with mildly reduced ejection fraction (fraction 44%) has ankle edema and breathlessness. Which therapy carries the strongest recommendation?","choices":["A mineralocorticoid antagonist","A neprilysin combination drug","A diuretic as needed","An evidence-based beta blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In mildly reduced ejection fraction, diuretics as needed carry the one strong recommendation. The sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor is moderate; the beta blocker, antagonist and renin-angiotensin drugs are weak. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman has a pressure of 104/64 at a routine visit and feels well. She worries because it is lower than her usual readings. What should she be told about this reading?","choices":["One reading is a snapshot in time","Pressure norms are fixed for all ages","It proves autonomic nerve failure","Any reading this low needs pressors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each reading is only a snapshot in time and is judged against age, comorbidities and the possibility of relative hypotension, together with symptoms. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has new swelling of both legs, heavy protein in her urine and low-density lipoprotein of 220 mg/dL. Before a statin is started, which condition should be ruled out?","choices":["Nephrotic syndrome","Metabolic syndrome","Familial chylomicronemia","Heterozygous familial form"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Elevated low-density lipoprotein calls for ruling out nephrotic syndrome, which drives excess very-low-density production and raises triglyceride and low-density lipoprotein. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has just been told she has heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia. She asks whether any single test can prove it. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["A skin biopsy proves it in her case","Fundoscopy settles the question","No definitive test exists for her form","A calcium score of zero rules it out"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. No definitive diagnostic test is available for the heterozygous form; the skin biopsy with receptor activity confirms only the homozygous disorder. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man fainted at home and has recovered. His electrocardiogram and glucose are normal. Which tests look for an infectious or metabolic contributor?","choices":["A blood count and metabolic panel","Orthostatic vitals only","A tilt-table test and a log","An echocardiogram and stress test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Unremarkable blood counts and a metabolic panel lower suspicion of an infectious or metabolic source, as glucose and the electrocardiogram address hypoglycemia and dysrhythmia. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with untreated severe aortic stenosis now has breathlessness on exertion, orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and ankle swelling. Without valve replacement, which average survival is expected from this symptom?","choices":["About 3 years","About 5 years","About 10 years","About 2 years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Of the cardinal symptoms, heart failure or exertional dyspnea is the most common and carries the worst prognosis, about 2 years. Angina carries about 5 years and syncope about 3 years. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 16, 22"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man reports lightheadedness and dimming vision within a minute or two of standing, which settles when he sits. His examination at rest is normal. Which test should be performed first?","choices":["Orthostatic vital signs","A stress test","A tilt-table test","An echocardiogram"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pressure and heart rate taken supine and again within 3 minutes of standing show whether orthostatic criteria are met and which type the heart rate response suggests. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with 20 years of type 2 diabetes has fainted twice shortly after getting out of bed. Her mucous membranes are moist, and vibration and light-touch sensation are reduced in both feet. Supine she is 140/80 with a heart rate of 70; after 3 minutes standing she is 110/66 with a heart rate of 74. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Vasovagal reflex syncope","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Cardiogenic arrhythmic syncope","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The systolic fall of 30 mmHg meets the criterion, the heart rate barely rises, and long-standing diabetes with neuropathy points to diabetic autonomic neuropathy. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with fatigue, cold intolerance and constipation is found to have blood pressure of 146/96 with a predominantly diastolic elevation. Which mechanism fits?","choices":["Autonomous aldosterone secretion","Catecholamine excess from an adrenal tumor","Hypothyroidism raising vascular resistance","Hyperthyroidism raising cardiac output"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypothyroidism raises systemic vascular resistance, which lifts the diastolic pressure, whereas hyperthyroidism raises cardiac output and lifts the systolic. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Left atrial enlargement in mitral stenosis predisposes to which rhythm?","choices":["Complete heart block","Atrial fibrillation","Ventricular tachycardia","Sinus bradycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rising left atrial size and pressure predispose to atrial fibrillation, which is a risk factor for thromboembolism and is common in severe mitral stenosis. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with hypertension has heart failure with a reduced ejection fraction and a myocardial infarction six months ago. Which class is preferred for him?","choices":["A beta blocker","A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","Hydralazine as monotherapy","Clonidine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are not first-line for uncomplicated hypertension, but reduced ejection fraction heart failure, recent infarction and ischemic disease are exactly the comorbidities that select them. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman has grade II diastolic dysfunction with an ejection fraction of 46%. She has fatigue and breathlessness with exertion but has never had pulmonary or peripheral edema and has never needed a diuretic. Does she meet a clinical diagnosis of congestive heart failure?","choices":["Yes, because she has symptoms","Yes, because her fraction is 46%","Yes, because she is older than 65","No, because congestion is required"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A clinical diagnosis of congestive heart failure requires congestion, meaning pulmonary or peripheral edema. She has symptoms and a mildly reduced ejection fraction, but without edema the congestive label does not apply. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman who smokes a pack of cigarettes a day asks whether smoking really matters for her heart at her age. Which counseling point is accurate?","choices":["It matters only with diabetes","It is a strong predictor at her age","It raises risk only in men","It matters only after menopause"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Smoking is a modifiable coronary risk factor and a strong predictor, especially in women younger than 45. Her age makes the counseling more urgent, not less. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has episodes of jaw and tooth pain when she walks uphill that ease when she rests. Her dentist found no dental cause. Which is the most likely cause of her pain?","choices":["Gallbladder disease","Pancreatitis","Aortic dissection","Myocardial ischemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exertional pain relieved by rest is ischemic, and acute coronary syndrome refers pain most commonly to the jaw, tooth or arm. Toothache is even listed among the descriptors of angina. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 31"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with chest pain has a nuclear stress test showing a perfusion defect that does not reverse at rest. What does this fixed defect indicate?","choices":["Prior infarction","A normal study","Coronary vasospasm","Reversible ischemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. On a nuclear stress test, a reversible perfusion defect indicates ischemia, while a non-reversible (fixed) defect indicates infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman without diabetes has low-density lipoprotein of 120 mg/dL and a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 3%. She has no risk-enhancing factors. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Moderate-intensity statin","Emphasize lifestyle","Ezetimibe alone","High-intensity statin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low risk (under 5%) calls for a risk discussion that emphasizes lifestyle to reduce risk factors, rather than a statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman's previously well-controlled blood pressure has risen over two months. She denies missing doses. What should the medication review specifically cover beyond her prescriptions?","choices":["Only agents started within the past week","Only intravenous medications given in hospital","Only her antihypertensive agents","Over-the-counter drugs, licorice and supplements"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and patients frequently do not volunteer any of these because they do not regard them as medication. A sudden loss of previously stable control is itself a clue to a secondary cause. Recreational substances belong in the same review. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man fell in the bathroom at 3 a.m. while urinating standing up, after two beers the evening before. He felt a swimmy head, nausea and sweating first and recovered quickly. His vital signs, neurologic examination and glucose are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Situational reflex syncope","Hypoglycemic loss of consciousness","Vasovagal reflex syncope"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nocturnal voiding after abruptly rising triggers an exaggerated autonomic reflex, and alcohol and relative dehydration add to it; this is micturition syncope. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction is stabilized and taken to the catheterization laboratory. Dye shows a tight narrowing of the anterior interventricular artery, which is opened with a balloon and held open with a drug-eluting stent. Which term names the procedure that opened the artery?","choices":["Percutaneous coronary intervention","Coronary angiography","Plain old balloon angioplasty","Left heart catheterization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Percutaneous coronary intervention is the placement of a drug-eluting or non-drug-eluting stent to keep the coronary artery open. Anything done to the vessel during the catheterization, beyond imaging it, is the intervention. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has chest pressure that began at rest. Her electrocardiogram shows T-wave inversion in two consecutive leads. Which result would make this a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["Pain that began at rest","Relief with nitroglycerin","Troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL","Inverted T waves persisting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Elevated cardiac markers (troponin or high-sensitivity troponin) are what separate a non-ST-elevation infarction from unstable angina. A standard troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL is abnormal. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is found to have a mid-systolic click at the apex at a sports physical. She feels well. Which is the most appropriate initial test?","choices":["Electrocardiogram","Echocardiogram","Chest radiograph","Cardiac catheterization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse is diagnosed by echocardiogram, which shows thickened, redundant mitral leaflets greater than 5 mm bowing into the left atrium during systole. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has fatigue and breathlessness with exertion. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 45%, and her fraction has never been lower. Which heart failure group describes her?","choices":["Reduced ejection fraction","Improved ejection fraction","Mildly reduced ejection fraction","Preserved ejection fraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 45% falls in the 41 to 49% band, and with no earlier value of 40% or less there is nothing to make it improved ejection fraction. Her treatment then resembles preserved ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis with leg swelling and jugular venous distension. Before any procedure, which medical regimen is appropriate?","choices":["Warfarin with a heparin bridge","Nitrates, beta blocker, calcium blocker","Low salt, diuretic, aldosterone antagonist","Afterload reduction with hydralazine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis is managed with a low-salt diet, diuretics and an aldosterone antagonist, with bioprosthetic valve replacement preferred when the valve is replaced. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man is discharged after receiving a mechanical aortic valve. Which medication must he be told he will take for life?","choices":["Warfarin","Nitroprusside","Heparin","Aspirin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A mechanical valve of titanium and carbon needs lifelong anticoagulation, and warfarin, the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist, is recommended for long-term management. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 75, 21"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man without cardiovascular disease has hypertension and smokes. His clinician wants to estimate his 10-year risk of atherosclerotic disease to guide statin therapy. Which tool is appropriate?","choices":["Pooled Cohort Equation","Metabolic syndrome criteria","Fundoscopic examination","Skin biopsy for receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It estimates 10-year primary risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease in patients 40 to 79 without pre-existing disease, using variables such as age, sex, cholesterol, systolic pressure, diabetes and smoking. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 25"}]};
